 ≥21𝑛⁢(𝑛𝑛/2) and hence that log⁡log⁡𝑚𝑛 ≥𝑛 −32⁢log⁡𝑛 −𝑂⁡(1). It had been conjectured since that the right answer is perhaps closer to Knuth's bound, see e.g. [8] and references therein. Recently, the authors [3] improved Piff's bound substantially and showed that log⁡log⁡𝑚𝑛 is within an additive 1 +𝑜⁡(1) term of Knuth's bound (instead of (1/2)⁢log⁡𝑛 +𝑂⁡(log⁡log⁡𝑛) as shown by Piff). In this note, we present an alternate proof of the fact that(2)log⁡log⁡𝑚𝑛≤𝑛−32⁢log⁡𝑛+log⁡log⁡𝑛+𝑂⁡(1).\n\nThis bound (2) differs from Knuth's lower bound by log⁡log⁡𝑛 +𝑂⁡(1) and hence is not as tight as the bound in [3]. However, the proof is much simpler and essentially follows by a direct entropy argument.\n\n## 2. Entropy\n\nFor a random variable X taking values in some finite set S, the entropy of X is defined as 𝐻⁡(𝑋) : =∑𝑥∈𝑆Pr⁡(𝑋 =𝑥)⁢log⁡1Pr⁡(𝑋=𝑥). For any such X, 𝐻⁡(𝑋) ≤log⁡|𝑆| with equality if and only if X is the uniformly random variable on S.\n\nThe following lemma is due to Shearer ([5]; see also [2] for a formal introduction to entropy and for a proof of the lemma).\n\nLemma 1\n\nLet 𝑋 =(𝑋1,𝑋2,…,𝑋𝑝) be a random variable taking values in the set 𝑆 =𝑆1 ×𝑆2 ×⋯ ×𝑆𝑝, where each of the coordinates 𝑋𝑖 of X is a random variable taking values in 𝑆𝑖. Let 𝒜 be a collection of subsets of [𝑝], such that each element of [𝑝] appears in at least k members of 𝒜. For 𝐴 ⊆[𝑝], let 𝑋𝐴 : =(𝑋𝑗)𝑗∈𝐴 (i.e. X restricted to coordinates in A). Then,(3)𝐻⁡(𝑋)≤1𝑘⁢∑𝐴∈𝒜𝐻⁡(𝑋𝐴)\n\nShearers' Lemma has many applications to counting problems (see [2], [11]). In this note, we will use Shearers' Lemma to bound the number of matroids.\n\n## 3. Application to matroids\n\nFor any set E and any 𝑟 ≤|𝐸|, we define ℳ𝐸,𝑟:={ℬ⊆(𝐸𝑟)∣⁢ℬsatisfies(1)}. If |𝐸| =𝑛, then |ℳ𝐸,𝑟| =𝑚𝑛,𝑟 +1, as ℳ𝐸,𝑟 contains the empty set in addition to the set of bases of each matroid of rank r on E. It will be convenient to view the elements ℬ of ℳ𝐸,𝑟 as (|𝐸|𝑟)-dimensional 0-1 indicator vectors, where each coordinate corresponds to an r-set of E.\n\nIf